WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.637
MARTIN OMANDER: Hi, Dina.

00:00:01.637 --> 00:00:02.970
DINA GRAVES PORTMAN: Hi, Martin.

00:00:02.970 --> 00:00:04.097
What's up?

00:00:04.097 --> 00:00:05.680
MARTIN OMANDER: In
a previous episode,

00:00:05.680 --> 00:00:09.090
you showed me your checklist
for reporting containerized web

00:00:09.090 --> 00:00:10.950
apps to Cloud Run.

00:00:10.950 --> 00:00:13.590
And by running them
serverlessly on Cloud Run,

00:00:13.590 --> 00:00:14.880
I got autoscaling.

00:00:14.880 --> 00:00:16.720
I only pay when my
code is running.

00:00:16.720 --> 00:00:19.183
And I don't have to
do server maintenance.

00:00:19.183 --> 00:00:20.850
DINA GRAVES PORTMAN:
Actually, I believe

00:00:20.850 --> 00:00:24.150
I showed you two checklists.

00:00:24.150 --> 00:00:25.860
MARTIN OMANDER: Oh, right.

00:00:25.860 --> 00:00:27.780
The first checklist
was for finding out

00:00:27.780 --> 00:00:31.050
if my containerized app
can be ported to Cloud Run.

00:00:31.050 --> 00:00:34.448
And it said that the
container should be stateless.

00:00:34.448 --> 00:00:35.865
DINA GRAVES PORTMAN:
That's right.

00:00:35.865 --> 00:00:37.710
MARTIN OMANDER: And
then in the comments,

00:00:37.710 --> 00:00:40.230
you were asked how to port
containerized web apps that

00:00:40.230 --> 00:00:42.750
store and use sessions--

00:00:42.750 --> 00:00:47.132
in other words, apps that
are stateful on the server.

00:00:47.132 --> 00:00:50.030
DINA GRAVES PORTMAN: OK, well,
those containers can still

00:00:50.030 --> 00:00:51.530
be ported to Cloud Run.

00:00:51.530 --> 00:00:54.390
But it does require
a few more steps.

00:00:54.390 --> 00:00:55.821
Let me show you how.

00:00:55.821 --> 00:00:58.226
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:01:05.459 --> 00:01:07.980
MARTIN OMANDER: All right, I
have this e-commerce web app

00:01:07.980 --> 00:01:10.380
that keeps track of the
user's shopping cart contents

00:01:10.380 --> 00:01:11.670
between requests.

00:01:11.670 --> 00:01:14.790
And it does so with
server side sessions.

00:01:14.790 --> 00:01:18.810
For example, if I reload
the page like this,

00:01:18.810 --> 00:01:21.900
the session is still active,
so the shopping cart content

00:01:21.900 --> 00:01:23.195
is still there.

00:01:23.195 --> 00:01:24.570
DINA GRAVES PORTMAN:
I see, yeah.

00:01:24.570 --> 00:01:26.820
That does make the
container stateful.

00:01:26.820 --> 00:01:30.120
Any time your application
reads and writes data locally,

00:01:30.120 --> 00:01:33.490
for instance, to a database
or a key value store,

00:01:33.490 --> 00:01:36.428
this means that your
application is stateful.

00:01:36.428 --> 00:01:38.220
And that's just a way
of saying that you're

00:01:38.220 --> 00:01:40.980
saving the state locally.

00:01:40.980 --> 00:01:44.955
But let's focus on the
user sessions right now.

00:01:44.955 --> 00:01:47.730
MARTIN OMANDER:
Yeah, why do we even

00:01:47.730 --> 00:01:50.212
need to think about
statefulness, Dina?

00:01:50.212 --> 00:01:52.170
DINA GRAVES PORTMAN:
Well, OK, here's the deal.

00:01:52.170 --> 00:01:55.890
If your server side code stores
user session data and memory,

00:01:55.890 --> 00:01:58.830
it's easy to lose it.

00:01:58.830 --> 00:02:00.080
MARTIN OMANDER: Why is that?

00:02:00.080 --> 00:02:01.840
DINA GRAVES PORTMAN:
Well, for instance,

00:02:01.840 --> 00:02:05.080
your server may crash,
which wipes out its memory.

00:02:05.080 --> 00:02:07.930
Also, if you're running your
app on a serverless platform,

00:02:07.930 --> 00:02:11.470
like Cloud Run,
each user request

00:02:11.470 --> 00:02:13.720
is sent to the next
available instance,

00:02:13.720 --> 00:02:16.330
which may not be
the instance that

00:02:16.330 --> 00:02:18.325
has the user session in memory.

00:02:18.325 --> 00:02:21.712
MARTIN OMANDER: Oh, that
sounds like a huge problem.

00:02:21.712 --> 00:02:23.170
DINA GRAVES PORTMAN:
Yeah, exactly.

00:02:23.170 --> 00:02:27.175
So try not to store sessions
in the server's memory.

00:02:27.175 --> 00:02:30.340
MARTIN OMANDER: OK, but I've
got to store the session data

00:02:30.340 --> 00:02:32.925
between requests somewhere.

00:02:32.925 --> 00:02:38.990
DINA GRAVES PORTMAN: Yes,
store it outside the server.

00:02:38.990 --> 00:02:42.380
For instance, Redis
is an open source

00:02:42.380 --> 00:02:44.000
distributed key value store.

00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:47.420
And it is a great
alternative to saving locally

00:02:47.420 --> 00:02:49.490
because it's fast
and it can still

00:02:49.490 --> 00:02:51.650
be reached by your
application servers.

00:02:51.650 --> 00:02:54.740
MARTIN OMANDER: OK, but my
app uses the Express framework

00:02:54.740 --> 00:02:57.350
with the Express
session package.

00:02:57.350 --> 00:03:01.633
And that package stores session
data in memory on the server.

00:03:01.633 --> 00:03:03.050
DINA GRAVES PORTMAN:
You're right.

00:03:03.050 --> 00:03:07.550
It does by default.
But you can edit it.

00:03:07.550 --> 00:03:11.180
You can add a Redis
connector to it.

00:03:11.180 --> 00:03:16.040
Many frameworks, like Express,
Django, Flask, Laravel, Spring

00:03:16.040 --> 00:03:19.010
Boot, Koa, Gin,
and Ruby on Rails,

00:03:19.010 --> 00:03:23.340
can be configured to use
Redis for session storage.

00:03:23.340 --> 00:03:24.210
MARTIN OMANDER: OK.

00:03:24.210 --> 00:03:27.510
Let me go and read the
docs for Express session

00:03:27.510 --> 00:03:30.240
and see if I can
connect it to Redis.

00:03:30.240 --> 00:03:32.705
Let's meet back here
for a little bit?

00:03:32.705 --> 00:03:34.080
DINA GRAVES PORTMAN:
Sounds good.

00:03:34.080 --> 00:03:35.670
I will grab a cup of tea.

00:03:39.420 --> 00:03:41.280
MARTIN OMANDER: All
right, I'm back, Dina.

00:03:41.280 --> 00:03:43.500
I added the Redis
connector to my app.

00:03:43.500 --> 00:03:45.900
It requires the address
of a Redis server

00:03:45.900 --> 00:03:47.760
and a port it listens to.

00:03:47.760 --> 00:03:49.680
So my code now
reads those values

00:03:49.680 --> 00:03:51.375
from environment variables.

00:03:51.375 --> 00:03:53.550
DINA GRAVES PORTMAN: Well done.

00:03:53.550 --> 00:03:56.580
So let's set up
your Redis server.

00:03:56.580 --> 00:04:01.180
Go to the Cloud Console,
open the hamburger menu,

00:04:01.180 --> 00:04:03.670
and click Memory Store.

00:04:03.670 --> 00:04:07.070
Memory Store is Google's
hosted solution for Redis,

00:04:07.070 --> 00:04:10.150
which automates many important
features like high availability

00:04:10.150 --> 00:04:11.840
and patching.

00:04:11.840 --> 00:04:13.420
MARTIN OMANDER: OK.

00:04:13.420 --> 00:04:16.610
I will pick Redis for now.

00:04:16.610 --> 00:04:21.130
And I'm enabling Memory
Store in this project.

00:04:21.130 --> 00:04:22.300
DINA GRAVES PORTMAN: Good.

00:04:22.300 --> 00:04:23.935
Now click Create Instance.

00:04:26.770 --> 00:04:30.870
Give it a name and
a display name.

00:04:30.870 --> 00:04:34.080
For the rest of the fields,
the defaults are fine.

00:04:34.080 --> 00:04:37.470
Now click Create.

00:04:37.470 --> 00:04:40.770
Now your Redis server
is being initialized.

00:04:40.770 --> 00:04:44.190
This server sits in a
virtual private cloud,

00:04:44.190 --> 00:04:47.650
so it's not accessible
from the public internet.

00:04:47.650 --> 00:04:50.280
So next, we need to
set up a connector so

00:04:50.280 --> 00:04:52.710
that your Cloud Run
service can connect

00:04:52.710 --> 00:04:56.010
to your virtual private cloud.

00:04:56.010 --> 00:04:59.400
To do that, first
pick VPC network

00:04:59.400 --> 00:05:02.190
in the navigation sidebar.

00:05:02.190 --> 00:05:06.410
Then click serverless
VPC access.

00:05:06.410 --> 00:05:13.910
Click Create Connector and give
it the name My VPC Connector.

00:05:13.910 --> 00:05:17.900
Under network, pick
Default. Under subnet,

00:05:17.900 --> 00:05:20.240
pick custom IP Range.

00:05:20.240 --> 00:05:25.310
And go ahead and enter that
suggested 10.8.0.0 in the IP

00:05:25.310 --> 00:05:28.615
text box and click Create.

00:05:31.350 --> 00:05:33.630
Now head back over
to the Memory Store

00:05:33.630 --> 00:05:35.850
and copy the IP address
of the Redis instance

00:05:35.850 --> 00:05:37.095
that you just created.

00:05:39.940 --> 00:05:44.140
In your deployment shell script,
add Redis host, Redis port,

00:05:44.140 --> 00:05:48.560
and VPC connector as
environment variables.

00:05:48.560 --> 00:05:49.550
MARTIN OMANDER: OK.

00:05:49.550 --> 00:05:55.850
Now that is done, I will build
and deploy the container.

00:05:55.850 --> 00:05:57.486
DINA GRAVES PORTMAN:
Is it working?

00:05:57.486 --> 00:05:59.070
MARTIN OMANDER: Let's see.

00:05:59.070 --> 00:06:01.590
Let me reload the page here.

00:06:01.590 --> 00:06:02.550
Refresh.

00:06:02.550 --> 00:06:04.620
Yeah, it's working.

00:06:04.620 --> 00:06:06.750
DINA GRAVES PORTMAN:
OK, awesome.

00:06:06.750 --> 00:06:09.870
Let's recap what
you have achieved.

00:06:09.870 --> 00:06:11.790
By moving your
container to Cloud Run,

00:06:11.790 --> 00:06:14.580
you no longer need
to maintain a server.

00:06:14.580 --> 00:06:18.600
And your service auto
scales as load increases.

00:06:18.600 --> 00:06:21.090
And we have Cloud Run
to thank for this.

00:06:21.090 --> 00:06:24.600
Now, Cloud Run is able to
offer these features by not

00:06:24.600 --> 00:06:26.670
storing the data in memory.

00:06:26.670 --> 00:06:31.080
So by putting the session data
in Redis via memory store,

00:06:31.080 --> 00:06:33.870
you won't lose it if the
user's second request hits

00:06:33.870 --> 00:06:36.480
a different server than
their first request

00:06:36.480 --> 00:06:39.165
or if an app server crashes.

00:06:39.165 --> 00:06:41.400
MARTIN OMANDER: I like that.

00:06:41.400 --> 00:06:44.190
Have we earned another
tea break, Dina?

00:06:44.190 --> 00:06:46.596
DINA GRAVES PORTMAN: I think so.

00:06:46.596 --> 00:06:48.420
MARTIN OMANDER: Thank
you all for watching.

00:06:48.420 --> 00:06:50.330
If you have any
questions for Dina or me,

00:06:50.330 --> 00:06:51.900
enter them in the
comments below,

00:06:51.900 --> 00:06:53.270
and we'll try to answer them.

00:06:53.270 --> 00:06:56.000
Also let us know what other
serverless topics we should

00:06:56.000 --> 00:06:57.800
bring up in this video series.

00:06:57.800 --> 00:06:58.940
Bye for now.

00:06:58.940 --> 00:07:01.990
[MUSIC PLAYING]

